  (text "TX AUDIO SELECT - relay switches what reaches the aircraft's mic\njack between ADAU1861's own analog output (AeroNode speaking) and\nthe pilot's boom mic (from the earcup), per Peter 2026-09-29.\n\nK1 = G6K-2F-Y (same qualified relay used on the H1 headset-mute\nboard - bifurcated crossbar contacts, rated for dry-circuit audio,\nNOT a generic silver-contact relay). Only pole A is used; pole B\n(pins 21/22/24) is unused and marked no-connect - see the isolation\nsection below for why HPOUTN isn't in this circuit at all.\n\nFAIL-PASSIVE CHOICE (mine, not yet confirmed by Peter): de-energized\n(NC, pin 12) = BOOM_MIC_FROM_EARCUP - the pilot's own mic still\nreaches the aircraft if power/control is lost. Energized (NO, pin\n14) = HPOUTP - AeroNode's audio reaches the aircraft mic input only\nwhen actively driven. Confirm this is the wanted default before fab.\n\nQ1/D1/R10 = standard low-side coil driver: GPIO_RLY_TX_SEL_3V3 (a\nnew signal on J1 pin 4, previously spare) -> 2N7002 gate, R10 100k\ngate pulldown for fail-passive (relay stays de-energized if the GPIO\nis high-Z during host boot), D1 flyback diode across the coil.\n\nRELAY COIL SUPPLY - RESOLVED per Peter 2026-09-29, option 1 of 4\nconsidered (see chat: dedicated regulator / PWM coil economiser /\ndifferent relay / borrow a 5V rail from aeronode). Fetched Omron's\nown G6K datasheet (not cached anywhere in this repo before now):\nmust-operate <=80% of rated, must-release >=10%, max voltage 150%\nof rated is EXPLICITLY INSTANTANEOUS ONLY, not continuous. Checked\nevery stock coil option (3/4.5/5/12/24 VDC) against VBAT 5.0-7.3V -\nnone spans it cleanly. The 5V coil is the only contender, but\nVBAT_max (7.3V = 146% of rated) held continuously is right at the\nedge of a rating the datasheet says not to run continuously.\n\nFIX: U11 (ADP7118AUJZ-1.8-R7 - the SAME part already used twice on\nthis board as U9/U10, just wired in its ADJUSTABLE configuration\ninstead of the fixed pass-through) regulates VBAT down to a fixed\n4.5V RLY_COIL_SUPPLY rail, independent of VBAT's swing entirely.\nR11 (15k)/R12 (10k) set VOUT = 1.8V*(1+R1/R2) = 4.5V, per the\nADP7118 datasheet's own adjustable-mode formula (Fig. 39). EN tied\nto VIN for auto-start, 2.2uF on VIN and VOUT per datasheet minimum -\nsame pattern as U9/U10's power note on the main sheet.\n\nAIRCRAFT-SIDE ISOLATION - CORRECTED 2026-09-29. T1 isolates ONLY\nthe AeroNode injection leg now, not the whole K1 common. Earlier\nthis session T1 sat between K1's common and J5, isolating BOTH the\nAeroNode path AND the boom-mic path from the aircraft. Peter caught\nthe consequence: \"bias will come from aircraft so DC must pass\nthrough to the boom mic\" - the aircraft supplies the electret\ncapsule's bias (8-16V, per the Bose A20 reference below) as a\ncomplete DC loop through Tip and Sleeve, and a transformer in that\nloop breaks the mic entirely, in both relay states, not just when\nAeroNode is talking.\n\nFix: T1 now sits between C38 and K1.14 (NO_A) ONLY - HPOUTP -> C38\n-> T1 primary -> T1 secondary -> R13 -> K1.14. K1.11 (COM_A) wires\nDIRECTLY to J5 Tip, no transformer in the way. This means:\n- De-energized (boom mic mode): J4 -> K1.12 -> K1.11 -> J5 Tip is a\n  fully direct, unisolated path. The aircraft's bias flows Tip ->\n  relay contact -> J4.1 -> capsule -> J4.2 -> Sleeve -> aircraft,\n  exactly as it would with no board in the way at all.\n- Energized (AeroNode mode): HPOUTP's signal crosses T1's isolation\n  boundary, through R13, through the (now-closed) relay contact, to\n  Tip. T1 still isolates AeroNode's own electronics from the\n  aircraft during this state - the isolation goal survives, just\n  scoped to the leg that actually needs it.\nThis ALSO means J4 (the boom mic connector) is electrically part of\nthe aircraft's circuit now, not AeroNode's: J4 pin 2 is wired to\nAC_GND, not AGND (changed this pass) - the capsule's return has to\ncomplete the SAME aircraft-side loop Sleeve does, or the bias never\nreaches it either. AGND and AC_GND still meet nowhere on this sheet\nexcept through T1's windings.\nThis CLOSES the \"boom mic gets no bias\" open item from two commits\nago outright, rather than carrying it forward - it was never a gap\nto size a local supply for, it was a routing mistake.\n\nSCAR: rerouting K1.11 to J5 Tip directly first auto-routed through\nthe exact point (185.42, 64.77) where J5's Sleeve/AC_GND stub\nalready landed - shorting Tip to AC_GND. find_shorted_nets caught\nit immediately (a visual check would not have - the two wires only\ntouched at one coordinate, easy to miss by eye); rerouted via a\ndifferent corner to clear it. Same class of bug as the collinear-\nwire shorts earlier in this sheet's history - checking net identity\nafter every reroute, not just after the first pass, is the lesson\nthat keeps needing relearning.\n\nT1 = Bourns SM-LP-5001, 600:600 1:1, same part H1's board uses, LCSC\nC7503474. Generic Device:Transformer_1P_1S symbol (pins 1-4) - H1's\nown note already flags the REAL part's pinout as 1/3/4/6 with 2/5\nN/C, a mismatch to resolve at footprint time, not assumed here.\n\nDRIVE CONFIGURATION - SINGLE-ENDED, per Peter 2026-09-29, changed\nfrom an earlier differential (HPOUTP+HPOUTN) attempt. Differential\nwould have matched H1's section 11.2 circuit exactly and is what\nthe ADAU186x Hardware Reference Manual (UG-2257) actually documents\nfor this output stage - it describes the headphone/line output as\ndifferential throughout, and unlike the analog INPUTS (which get an\nexplicit single-ended mode and an AINx_INPUT_MODE register bit),\nthere is no equivalent single-ended output mode anywhere in the\nmanual or the HP_CTRL register. Single-ended HPOUTP use is\ntherefore an undocumented configuration - flagged, not resolved.\n\nC38 (2.2uF) is in series with HPOUTP, and it is NOT there for the\nold reason (blocking the aircraft's 8-16V mic bias - T1 already\ndoes that unconditionally for this leg). It blocks a different DC:\nHPOUTP sits at the DAC's ~0.85V common-mode bias, and tying T1's\nother primary leg to AGND (instead of HPOUTN) would otherwise push\nthat 0.85V across the winding continuously - roughly 0.85V/115R\n(winding DCR) = 7.4mA of DC bias current toward core saturation.\nC38 removes that DC path. 2.2uF against the ~1.2k reflected\nsecondary-side load (R13+DCR+the assumed aircraft bias resistor)\ngives a corner around 60 Hz, below the voice band. X7R is fine here\n(not film/C0G like the old C37) - the DC bias across this cap is\nonly ~0.85V, nowhere near the 16V that made X7R derating a real\nproblem on H1's aircraft-facing capacitor.\n\nD2 (BAT54S) KEPT on HPOUTP's own node (before C38), clamping the IC\npin directly between AGND and 1V8 - \"belt-and-braces\" per H1's own\nframing, since T1's isolation is what actually stands between the\ncodec and any aircraft-side fault now, not this clamp.\n\nLEVEL/IMPEDANCE MATCHING - R13 changed from 10k to 470R, 2026-09-29.\n10k was copied from H1's R1 without checking it against what this\nmic actually looks like. The already-fetched reference for this\nexact boom mic (Bose A20, docs/h1-headset-mute-relay.md): bias\n8-16 VDC through 220-2200 ohm, sensitivity 600 mV rms at 114 dB SPL.\nThat 220-2200R IS the mic's own source impedance as seen from the\naircraft's mic input. R13=10k made our path present ~10.1k - 20-40x\nhigher than a real mic, and (at the old level) only 35 mV rms at the\nmic node, 24.7 dB under the mic's rated max.\nR13=470R (matching the \"typical\" 470R already assumed elsewhere in\nthis design, same placeholder H1 used) puts our presented impedance\nat ~585R (470+115 DCR, the relay contact's 100mR max is negligible\nin series) - inside the mic's real 220-2200R range. Level: single-\nended HPOUTP (0.5V rms) x T1's 2dB insertion loss x the\n470/(470+115+470) divider = ~177 mV rms at the mic node, -10.6dB\nunder the 600mV/114dB SPL max - much closer than the old 35mV, and\nconsistent with going single-ended (6dB under the differential\ncase's ~352mV). [assumed] the 470R aircraft bias resistor is a\nplaceholder within the stated 220-2200R range, not measured. This\nmath is unchanged by moving T1/R13 to before the relay instead of\nafter it - the contact it now transits is negligible resistance.\n\nFIRMWARE, not a schematic item but decided alongside this circuit:\nHP_MODE (bit 0 of HP_CTRL, address 0x4000C060) should be set to 1\n(line-out mode) for this path, not left at its 0 (headphone mode)\nreset default. Headphone mode is rated for >=10R loads; line-out\nfor >=10k. T1's primary presents ~7-72k of inductive reactance\nacross the voice band (3.8H) - a line-out load, not a headphone one.\n\nOPEN ITEMS - not resolved by this pass:\n1. J5 Ring: NO-CONNECT. The Tip/Sleeve loop now carries the\n   aircraft's bias to the boom mic directly, so Ring has no defined\n   role either way - leave it NC unless the real PJ068 adapter cable\n   needs it for something else.\n2. J4 and J5 are generic placeholders, same convention as J1/J2/J3\n   on the main sheet - real connectors once the mechanical interface\n   is chosen.\n3. T1 (SM-LP-5001) is NOT a flight part - same disclosure as H1's\n   note: -20 to +85C operating range (a cold-soaked GA cockpit can\n   go below that) and UL60950 (IT-equipment), not DO-160. Right for\n   a bench/proof-of-concept board, wrong for a flight article without\n   requalification - a sourcing exercise nobody has started.\n4. Single-ended HPOUTP drive is outside the documented operating\n   mode for this output stage (see DRIVE CONFIGURATION above). C38\n   handles the DC consequence; it does not make this a spec'd mode.\n5. U11's aggregate load on VBAT is trivial (21mA) and doesn't touch\n   the U10/3V3_ACCEL budget flagged in the power note - a genuinely\n   separate rail, deliberately, so the relay coil can never brown\n   out the accelerometer/translator stack or vice versa."
    (at 420 200 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)))
    (uuid "9a98c05e-855d-4ccf-a2b6-1cabc154f8fb")
